description:
File: rust/lichen-rpl/src/multi_instance.rs
Lines: 346-360

The get_role() method has a time-of-check-time-of-use race condition:

```rust
let has_peers = {
    let peers = self.peers.lock().expect("peers mutex poisoned");
    !peers.is_empty()
};
// Lock released here - another thread can modify peers

if !has_peers {
    return GatewayRole::Standalone;
}

// Another thread could remove all peers between check and use
let master = self.elect_time_master();
```

Trigger: Thread A calls get_role(), checks has_peers=true, releases lock. Thread B calls remove_peer() for all peers. Thread A calls elect_time_master() which now returns None (no local, no peers), but get_role() proceeds assuming peers exist.

Bad behavior: elect_time_master() returns None, causing match to return Secondary instead of Standalone. Role determination is inconsistent with actual federation state.

Fix: Hold the peers lock across the entire role determination, or redesign to use a single atomic operation.
